  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 1249 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nTuesday Night into Wednesday, we continue to watch a wave track \nsoutheast through the Central CONUS, bringing increasing cloud cover \nand precipitation chances to the area. Confidence on rainfall \nremains low-medium, owing to uncertainty amongst guidance on \nmoisture availability. Soundings would favor the potential for light \nrainfall, but saturation may take some time. We continue to see \nwidespread 30-50% PoPs through the day, with best chances in the \nafternoon east of the Mississippi River. While the chances may be \nmore widespread, we are not expecting widespread heavy rainfall.\nMost locations may get through the day without seeing much, if any, \nrainfall. Rainfall accumulation is expected to be lower as well, \nwhere those that see rain can expect light accumulations <0.10\". \nOtherwise, a mostly cloudy day can be expected with temperatures in \nthe mid-upper 50s. \n\nWednesday Night-Sunday, another shortwave to drop south in the \nnorthwest flow aloft on Thursday. Guidance has trended down on the \nrainfall potential compared to the last forecast package, leading to \nlittle/no PoPs. If we manage to see any precipitation, it will \nlikely be isolated showers passing through the cyclonic flow over \nthe region, but that is low confidence. What is of higher \nconfidence, is the colder air arriving behind this wave. High \ntemperatures to drop closer if not a few degrees cooler than normal \ninto the low 40s Thursday and Friday, especially if clouds stick \naround longer. After Friday, a large area of high pressure and a \nbuilding upper ridge over the central CONUS will bring another \nperiod of dry conditions and above normal temperatures.",
